 "old_string": "## Confirmed rule\nFor the tested variant, the grid contains a dominant background color and sparse contrasting cells. Recolor every cell of the dominant color to the sparse color, and every sparse-color cell to 0. Preserve dimensions and positions; this is a global palette remap, not a spatial pattern transformation.",
 "new_string": "## Confirmed rule\nFor the demonstrated variant, the grid contains exactly two colors, with 2 as the dominant background and sparse 5 markers. Recolor every 2 to 0, and every 5 to 2. Preserve dimensions and positions; this is a global palette remap, not a spatial pattern transformation. In a fresh instance, use the demo to identify which actual input color is mapped to 0 and which to the output marker value; do not infer the mapping solely from which input color is numerically larger or less frequent.\n\nA related instance with a noisy 4/5 two-color field had 5 as the more frequent color and 4 less frequent, but its accepted output was 5→0 and 4→5 (the reverse of the initial frequency-based guess). This is an important warning: frequency alone does not identify the mapping direction, even when the grid is near-balanced or one color is modestly dominant. Request another demo rather than submitting the intuitive complement when output colors are ambiguous."
